</p></sec><sec id="Sec11"><title>MIP over the stay</title><p id="Par33">The evolution in MIP during the hospital stay is illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref> for the study population and for the two subgroups with and without persistent IMW at three timepoints: Inclusion, ICU discharge and study completion.</p><p id="Par34">The random forest model identified MIP at day 12 as the optimal early timepoint for predicting persistent IMW at study completion. At day 12, MIP was 24 [17&#x02013;41] cmH<sub>2</sub>O for the study population, with values of 17 [13&#x02013;23] cmH&#x02082;O and 38 [24&#x02013;50] cmH&#x02082;O recorded in patients with and without persistent IMW, respectively.</p><p id="Par35">
<fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>MIP over the stay, Values of Maximal Inspiratory Pressure (MIP) at inclusion, Intensive Care Unit discharge and study completion, in the study population and by subgroups with and without persistent Inspiratory Muscle Weakness (MIP&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;30 cmH2O) at study completion. Only patients with an evaluable MIP value are displayed in the figure</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="13054_2025_5718_Fig2_HTML" id="d33e1458"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec12"><title>MRC scores over the stay</title><p id="Par36">The evolution in MRC score is illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref> for the study population and by subgroups with and without persistent IMW at three timepoints: Inclusion, ICU discharge and study completion. The value of MRC score at day 12 was 44 [36&#x02013;51] for the study population, respectively 37 [31&#x02013;45] and 44 [42&#x02013;54] for the subgroups with and without persistent IMW.</p><p id="Par37">
<fig id="Fig3"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>MRC score over the stay, Values of Medical Research Council (MRC) score at inclusion, Intensive Care Unit discharge and study completion, in the study population and by subgroups with and without persistent Inspiratory Muscle Weakness (MIP&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;30 cmH2O) at study completion. Only patients with an evaluable MRC score value are displayed in the figure</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="13054_2025_5718_Fig3_HTML" id="d33e1475"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec13"><title>Factors associated with persistent IMW at study completion</title><p id="Par38">Female sex, duration of catecholamine use prior to inclusion, MIP at day 12, MRC score at day 12, changes in MIP from inclusion to day 12, and ICU-acquired weakness diagnostic documented in the medical file showed a significant positive association with persistent with persistent IMW. The presence of a respiratory comorbidity (e.g. known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, interstitial lung disease or asthma) was negatively associated with persistent IMW.</p><p id="Par39">To further explore the robustness of the findings, a post-hoc sensitivity analysis using univariable linear regression was conducted to assess the relationship between the MIP value at study completion and the variables used for the logistic regression (see Additional file 3).</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec14"><title>Discussion</title><p id="Par40">The natural progression of inspiratory muscle strength was investigated among patients presenting with IMW defined as MIP&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;30 cmH<sub>2</sub>O after prolonged MV (&#x02265;&#x02009;7 days). At study completion, 43% of patients had persistent IMW, and 57% had an MRC score&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;48/60, corresponding to the threshold of ICU-AW. In univariable logistic regression, persistent IMW at study completion was positively associated with female sex, duration of catecholamine use until inclusion, MIP and MRC scores at day 12, and changes in MIP from inclusion to day 12. Among these, MIP at day 12 showed the highest predictive value, with its change from inclusion also significantly associated with the outcome.</p><p id="Par41">To the authors&#x02019; knowledge, this study is the first to longitudinally document the spontaneous evolution of inspiratory muscle strength during acute care in patients invasively ventilated for &#x02265;&#x02009;7 days. It shows that persistent IMW is frequent and reveals its heterogeneous recovery trajectories, distinguishing patients with spontaneous recovery from those with persistent IMW.</p><p id="Par42">In prior research the occurrence of IMW is associated with several risk factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR12">12</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref>]. Among these, this study found only catecholamine use to be associated with persistent IMW. No significant association was found between total MV duration and persistent IMW, suggesting that recovery depends on other factors than MV duration when it exceeds 7 days [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>]. Interestingly, patients with documented respiratory comorbidity were more likely to recover inspiratory muscle strength, possibly due to tailored management. This however must be confirmed by further dedicated studies.</p><p id="Par43">This study highlights the difficulty of predicting persistent IMW based on patient characteristics, ICU admission reasons, or treatments received, and underscores the importance of repeated MIP assessments to better identify those at risk. According to the present data, measurement of MIP at day 12 appears to be a relevant clinical opportunity to identify the patients who are at high risk of having persistent IMW. Patients identified at this stage may benefit from targeted interventions, such as Inspiratory Muscle Training (IMT), which is both feasible and safe in the ICU setting [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR30">30</xref>]. Early targeted and personalized rehabilitation strategies may be especially valuable as IMW, similarly to ICU-AW, is related to poor outcomes beyond acute care, including the Post-Intensive Care Syndrome, affecting up to 56% of ICU survivors one year after discharge and being associated with reduced quality of life [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref>]. Both respiratory and peripheral weakness worsen prognosis, increasing mortality and reducing independence. In the present, consistent with previous findings [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>], IMW was significantly associated with hospital mortality. Early identification and tailored interventions may thus improve both short- and long-term outcomes, including survival [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR34">34</xref>].</p><p id="Par44">This study has several strengths, namely its prospective design and the use of simple bedside evaluations of muscle strength. Unlike previous studies that assessed IMW exclusively at the time of weaning or sedation withdrawal [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR17">17</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>], the present study documents muscle recovery with repeated measurements throughout acute care. This longitudinal data collection highlights the existence of patients with persistent IMW and provides a timeframe to prospectively identify those at-risk.</p><p id="Par45">Several limitations require consideration. First, poor patient cooperation and coordination may affect measurement reliability&#x02014;particularly in case of delirium or reduced consciousness shortly after extubation. In this cohort, MIP was not feasible in 9% of evaluations, mostly (75%) within the first five days after inclusion, underscoring the need for repeated assessments. Second, the study included only patients with a MIP&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;30 cmH&#x02082;O within 48&#x000a0;h post-extubation, excluding those with higher initial values and thus evaluated only a subpopulation of ICU patients with documented inspiratory muscle weakness. Third, follow-up was limited to the acute care hospital stay in the participating hospitals, and early discharges may have led to overestimating the prevalence of persistent IMW at study completion. Fourth, variability in ICU discharge criteria across centres may have influenced assessment timings. Fifth, the relatively small sample size may have limited the statistical power and generalizability. Given the sample size, multivariable regression and statistical analysis that specifically account for time were deemed inappropriate. Finally, pre-admission nutritional status&#x02014;a known determinant of muscle recovery&#x02014;was not analysed and may have influenced the results.</p></sec><sec id="Sec15"><title>Conclusion</title><p id="Par46">This study highlights the high prevalence of persistent IMW throughout the acute care stay among patients with critical illness who experienced prolonged intubation. Factors positively associated in univariable logistic regression with persistent inspiratory muscle weakness were female sex, duration of catecholamine use until inclusion, MIP at day 12, MRC score at day 12 and changes in MIP from inclusion to day 12. This suggests that early identification of patients at risk of persistent muscle weakness is feasible using MIP measurement and could be used to tailor and individualize rehabilitation strategies for these patients who experience poor long-term outcomes. Future research is essential to confirm the results of the present study and to assess the potential impact on long-term outcomes of a systematic early detection of patients at risk of persistent IMW coupled with individualized rehabilitation programs.</p></sec><sec id="Sec16" sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Information</title><p>
